CLAIMS=[
('The average person in the US spends close to $4,000 a year eating out.','spends close to $4,000 a year eating',P+'BLS Consumer Expenditures 2024 (USDL-25-1586, released 2025-12-19, before the 2026-08-29 upload): food away from home $3,945 per consumer unit. The figure matches, but it is per household (consumer unit), not per person.',['BLSCE277']),
('Redirecting $3,500 a year at a 10% annual return for 30 years turns into north of $600,000.','That turns into something north of $600,000',C+'Year-end annual contributions give $575,729 (below $600,000); start-of-year contributions give $633,302 and monthly contributions ($291.67 at 10%/12) give $659,309. "North of $600,000" holds only under the latter conventions; 10% is a nominal return, so the real value is far lower.',[]),
('A 22% card makes paying it off a guaranteed 22% return; handle debt above ~8-9% before aggressive investing.','paying off a 22% card is mathematically a guaranteed',R+'Fed G.19 average card APRs were ~21-22% in 2025-2026 by recall; not fetched. The payoff-as-return logic is correct (after-tax, risk-free equivalent). The 8-9% threshold is a common rule of thumb.',[]),
('A 50% employer match is an immediate guaranteed 50% return.','that\'s an immediate guaranteed 50%',C+'A 50% match on each contributed dollar is a 50% immediate gain (subject to vesting), so the arithmetic is correct. Vesting schedules are not mentioned.',[]),
('The avalanche method (highest rate first) saves the most; the snowball (smallest balance first) aids motivation.','The avalanche method has you attacking',R+'Correct: avalanche minimises interest by construction; Gal and McShane (2012, "small victories", Kellogg) support snowball motivation by recall.',[]),
('A caller with $40,000 mostly in single stocks was told to cap any single stock at ~10% of net worth.','had over $40,000 sitting mostly in single stocks',R+'Unnamed finance-show anecdote; not located. The 10% concentration cap is a common guideline. '+U,[]),
('Priority order: employer match, high-interest debt, small emergency fund, then investing.','Contribute enough to grab the full employer match',R+'Standard sequencing used by many planners; it places the emergency fund after debt payoff, whereas many guides put a starter fund first. Advice, not a factual claim.',[]),
]
TITLE='Authentic title "How To Build Wealth If You\'re Already Behind" is a how-to with a reassurance frame for late starters. The script delivers that (net worth check, leaks, income, debt, match, automation). TYPICAL against the 10-peer cohort (0.567x) but WEAK on the 5-peer sensitivity check (0.493x), so the tier is unstable; the video was only ~13 days old at the metric snapshot. Association only.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White background. Left: the recurring cartoon man in a white polo, calm half-smile, raising an index finger. Right: a heavy black two-line headline "DON\'T FALL BEHIND..." above a green four-bar rising chart with a black upward arrow and a pink piggy bank with a gold dollar coin. At 320x180 the headline, bars and piggy bank stay legible. The warning wording ("Don\'t fall behind") differs from the title\'s audience (people already behind).'
SCRIPT='A catch-up plan: hook (38-year-old vs 24-year-old co-worker); highlight-reel comparison; not starting is the costliest mistake; step one net worth; contribution rate and consistency over start date ($3,500/yr eating-out redirect at 10% for 30 years); income lever over cutting; debt sequencing (22% card, avalanche vs snowball); employer match; priority order; risk-chasing mistake (caller anecdote, 10% single-stock cap); late-starter at 45 using index funds; automation and raising contributions; tree-planting proverb; recap and community link. Verified: eating-out ~$4,000 (BLS $3,945 per household). The $600,000 figure holds only with monthly or start-of-year contributions ($575,729 year-end). Mostly advice; few checkable claims. The stored ending is complete.'
